WEBVTT

00:00:08.679 --> 00:00:12.931
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Covering over 2,300 square kilometres of north-west England,

00:00:13.268 --> 00:00:17.600
the Lake District National Park boasts some of the country’s most impressive scenery.

00:00:23.236 --> 00:00:28.317
And it’s easy to see why it attracts approximately 20 million visitors each year.

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:37.120
Many of these tourists come here to satisfy their thirst for adventure, to battle the elements,

00:00:38.736 --> 00:00:44.283
to hike across the region’s many peaks, and enjoy the epic landscape.

00:00:45.737 --> 00:00:48.000
But no adventure comes without risk.

00:00:49.031 --> 00:00:52.520
And if you get into trouble, you’ll need one of the area’s dedicated

00:00:52.542 --> 00:00:54.769
Mountain Rescue Teams to help you out.

00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:00.240
There are 12 different Mountain Rescue Teams across the Lake District

00:01:00.389 --> 00:01:03.812
and they operate all year round, whatever the conditions.

00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:10.520
It’s one of the busiest regions in the country, with around 600 call-outs in the last year,

00:01:12.046 --> 00:01:16.738
and there are over 450 Rescue Team members operating across the region.

00:01:18.324 --> 00:01:21.219
But who are they and what exactly do they do?

00:01:22.558 --> 00:01:25.890
We spoke to members of two different teams to find out.

00:01:28.819 --> 00:01:30.422
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> My name is Gordon Barker.

00:01:30.598 --> 00:01:33.994
I live in Keswick and I’m the chairman of Keswick Mountain Rescue Team.

00:01:34.019 --> 00:01:36.035
I’ve been the chairman for 5 years.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:40.021
<v><b>David:</b></v> My name is David Stewart and I’m the deputy team leader

00:01:40.048 --> 00:01:43.333
with Kirkby Stephen Mountain Rescue Team, which is in Cumbria.

00:01:43.836 --> 00:01:47.233
I’ve been in Kirkby Stephen Mountain Rescue Team for 12 years now,

00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:50.149
and I’ve been a deputy team leader for 4 years.

00:01:51.436 --> 00:01:55.080
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> A Mountain Rescue Team is responsible, essentially,

00:01:55.105 --> 00:02:01.722
for responding to any kind of injury or medical calamity that might happen in the outdoors.

00:02:02.269 --> 00:02:04.800
Er, we don’t have a professional service to do that.

00:02:05.247 --> 00:02:10.567
And so, it relies on volunteers to jump in Land Rovers, drive to the bottom of a hill,

00:02:10.923 --> 00:02:16.505
and then run up that hill as quickly as you can to bring first aid and whatever other medical, er,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.530
or other care that is needed by the casualty on scene.

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.509
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> So how exactly does this work?

00:02:24.378 --> 00:02:28.320
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> Generally, the way it works is the first couple of people to arrive on scene,

00:02:28.708 --> 00:02:30.530
they’re always the fittest in the truck.

00:02:30.555 --> 00:02:35.005
Er, there’s a little selection process right at the start: who’s fit? Who’s quickest?

00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:37.964
Right, they take the, you know, the first-aid sack,

00:02:37.973 --> 00:02:40.620
which contains all the immediate care stuff they need.

00:02:41.477 --> 00:02:45.821
Er, and, generally, they focus on the casualty.

00:02:46.421 --> 00:02:49.728
And then, more team members arrive from the second truck as well.

00:02:49.753 --> 00:02:53.000
And then, you know, the casualty’s being treated,

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:55.937
they’re getting pain relief, maybe their injuries are being seen to

00:02:56.478 --> 00:03:01.760
and just making sure the casualty’s kept warm and safe and, and can be packaged efficiently.

00:03:05.422 --> 00:03:08.703
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> It’s highly pressurized work under very tough conditions.

00:03:09.018 --> 00:03:11.960
So, why do people volunteer to do it?

00:03:13.284 --> 00:03:19.480
<v><b>Gordon:</b></v> It’s hard to explain really now, but, if I’m honest, ultimately, it’s fun.

00:03:20.245 --> 00:03:24.017
You know, it, it’s two in the morning, the text’s just come in,

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.454
and it’s throwing it down with rain outside and it’s black outside and it’s…

00:03:29.078 --> 00:03:31.004
the wind is rattling the windows…

00:03:31.275 --> 00:03:35.895
and there’s a part of you just wants to stay warm and cosy in bed and just ignore it.

00:03:36.283 --> 00:03:40.946
But at the same time, you know that there’s this crazy bunch of people getting out of bed,

00:03:40.973 --> 00:03:46.643
legging it down to base and getting all that kit on and preparing to go out in that tempest.

00:03:46.677 --> 00:03:48.800
And, and you don’t want to miss out.

00:03:52.537 --> 00:03:56.557
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Mountain Rescue Teams aren’t just about emergency rescue operations.

00:03:56.873 --> 00:04:01.200
They also offer lots of advice about walking safely in the mountains.

00:04:04.145 --> 00:04:06.760
<v><b>David:</b></v> If you’re going for an adventure in the hills or the mountains,

00:04:07.023 --> 00:04:12.600
there are really three key areas in which you need to prepare. The first is kit.

00:04:13.132 --> 00:04:15.800
You need to have the right equipment for what you’re going to do.

00:04:17.986 --> 00:04:20.600
The second key area is skills.

00:04:21.784 --> 00:04:25.855
It’s all very well having the right equipment, having a map and compass, for instance.

00:04:26.491 --> 00:04:31.520
But they’re no good if you don’t know how to use them. The third key area is the weather.

00:04:32.489 --> 00:04:37.064
The weather can really affect what you can do during a day and how enjoyable it is.

00:04:37.468 --> 00:04:41.915
Fortunately, there are lots of good tools, lots of good weather forecasting sites

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.013
and apps where you can keep right up to date on what the weather is going to do.